    "english_commentary": "Chapter 7 inaugurates the 'Bhakti' (devotional) section of the Bhagavad Gita, transitioning from the mechanics of meditation (Chapter 6) to the profound theology of the Absolute. Having declared the Bhakti-yogi as the highest in verse 6.47, Krishna now reveals exactly 'Who' the yogi is meditating upon. Krishna sets three prerequisites for ultimate spiritual realization: 'mayy aasakta-manaah' (the mind must be intensely, lovingly attached to Him), 'mad-aashrayah' (the practitioner must take complete shelter in Him rather than relying on their own strength), and 'yogam yunjan' (the consistent practice of this connection). By fulfilling these conditions, the practitioner will achieve something extraordinary: they will know the Supreme Lord 'samagram' (completely, in full detail) and 'asamshayam' (absolutely free from any speculative doubt).",
